INT. BOXING GYM - ABOVE A FUNERAL PARLOR - EVENING

A long upstairs room with warped floorboards. One heavy bag, patched with duct tape. A ring with sagging ropes. A window facing a hearse garage; through the glass, a black Cadillac gleams.

EMEKA OKONKWO (30s, heavyweight, taped hands, hooded sweatshirt) hits the bag in slow, deliberate pairs. LARS VESTERGAARD (50s, trainer, gray crew cut, a towel on his shoulder) holds a pair of mitts and watches him with a steady, flat gaze.

LARS
Again.

EMEKA
I've done fifty.

LARS
Fifty is warm-up.

EMEKA
Fifty is my shoulder.

LARS
Your shoulder is fine.

Emeka lets his arms fall. He walks to the window and looks down at the hearse. He wipes sweat off his face with the hem of his sweatshirt.

EMEKA
Why did you rent above a funeral home?

LARS
It was cheap.

EMEKA
Is that the reason?

LARS
It's quiet.

EMEKA
Nobody downstairs complains about the noise?

LARS
Nobody downstairs complains about anything.

Emeka almost smiles. He returns to the bag and sets his feet. He hits it twice, hard, and the chain squeaks.

LARS (CONT'D)
Better. Where did that come from?

EMEKA
From you telling me I'm too slow.

LARS
I never said that.

EMEKA
You said it with your eyebrows.

LARS
My eyebrows have no opinion.

Emeka hits again. And again. A faster rhythm builds, the old chain snapping, the tape on the bag peeling a little at its seam. Lars lifts the mitts and Emeka drives his fists into them, one-two, one-two, until the older man's knees begin to bend.

LARS (CONT'D)
Enough.

EMEKA
No.

LARS
Enough, I said.

EMEKA
Three more.

LARS
Fine. Three.

Emeka lands three, and they are enormous. Lars staggers. He drops the mitts, rubs his palms together, shakes out his wrists.

LARS (CONT'D)
That's what I wanted.

EMEKA
Why wait until now?

LARS
Because you had to be angry.

EMEKA
I wasn't angry.

LARS
You were furious at me.

EMEKA
I was tired.

LARS
It's the same thing. Both of them look like the end.

Emeka sits on the ring apron. He unwinds the tape from one hand, slowly. Lars pours him water from a thermos, then pours himself some, then sits, and the two of them listen to the hearse engine turn over below.

EMEKA
Fight's on Saturday.

LARS
I know.

EMEKA
You think I'm ready?

LARS
I think he's not.

EMEKA
Who, him or you?

LARS
Either. Both. The one standing in front of you.

Emeka laughs. Lars does not, but something in his shoulders eases.

EMEKA
If I lose.

LARS
Then you lose here, with me, and nobody downstairs will notice.

EMEKA
Thanks.

LARS
You're welcome.

The hearse pulls out of the garage and glides into the street, slow and silent.
